  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather continues through the weekend and much \nof next week. Cool today, with afternoon temperatures gradually \nmoderating this weekend, though chilly mornings will bring \nwidespread frost to much of the area away from the immediate coast.\n\nAs anticipated, Subtropical Storm Josephine has undergone \nextratropical transition, now Post-Tropical Cyclone Josephine per \nthe National Hurricane Center. This system is moving east and will \npass to the south of Nova Scotia and New Brunswick Friday into \nSaturday. With the center of the system moving further east into the \nNorth Atlantic Ocean, expansive high pressure builds across the \nNortheast. Thus, any lingering gusts of 20 to 30 mph will be \nconfined to the easternmost waters and adjacent coastal areas, \nparticularly from Cape Ann to Cape Cod and the Islands. Winds \ndiminish late this morning into the afternoon, while areas farther \ninland will be treated to a pleasant day with light northerly winds \nof 5 to 10 mph. Full sunshine today with highs ranging from the \nlower to middle 50s. \n\nLooking ahead, there really isn't any sensible weather to discuss as \nthe aforementioned high pressure system remains the dominant weather \nfeature for the foreseeable future. However, a weak cold front \nstarved of moisture approaches from the north-northwest on Tuesday. \nStrong high pressure and dry air will likely cause this front to dry \nout before reaching southern New England, perhaps adding some cloud \ncover at most. In fact, global ensembles paint a rather parched \npicture for southern New England, with probabilities of measurable \nprecipitation (0.01 inches or greater) generally less than 10 \npercent from Friday through Wednesday of next week.\n\nIn terms of temperatures, expect moderating afternoon temperatures \nthis weekend, returning to the upper 50s/lower 60s on Saturday and \nlow to middle 60s on Sunday and Monday. Saturday and Sunday mornings \nwill be quite cold, with lows dropping into the 20s to lower 30s and \nwidespread frost likely away from the immediate coast. Be sure to \nprotect those mums and Halloween pumpkins! Opted to trend overnight \nlows below NBM guidance, which tends to run too warm during \nefficient radiational cooling setups, especially with clear skies, \ncalm winds, and strong 1030+ mb surface high pressure overhead. \nTuesday into the upcoming week seasonable temperatures day and night \nare expected.",
